                            %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/derived+stack"} derived stack
                  %h2#theorems Theorems
                  %ul
                    %li
                      %p
                        %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/Diaconescu%27s+theorem"} Diaconescu's theorem
                    %li
                      %p
                        %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/Barr%27s+theorem"} Barr's theorem
                  %div
                    %p
                      %a{:href => "/nlab/edit/topos+theory+-+contents"} Edit this sidebar
          %h1#contents Contents
          .maruku_toc
            %ul
              %li
                %a{:href => "#definition"} Definition
              %li
                %a{:href => "#properties"} Properties
              %li
                %a{:href => "#examples"} Examples
              %li
                %a{:href => "#the_connection_with_propositional_logic"} The connection with propositional logic
              %ul
                %li
                  %a{:href => "#the_initial_and_terminal_toposes_as_classifying_toposes"} The initial and terminal toposes as classifying toposes
              %li
                %a{:href => "#generalizations"} Generalizations
              %li
                %a{:href => "#related_concepts"} Related concepts
              %li
                %a{:href => "#references"} References
          %h2#definition Definition
          %p
            In intrinsic terms, a
            %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/topos"} topos
            is
            %em localic
            if it is generated under
            = succeed "s" do
              %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/colimit"} colimit
            = succeed "s" do
              %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/subobject"} subobject
            %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/terminal+object"} terminal object
            = succeed "." do
              %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "inline", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
                %semantics
                  %mrow
                    %mn 1
                  %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"} 1
          %p
            In equivalent but extrinsic terms, a category is a localic topos if it is equivalent to the
            %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/category+of+sheaves"} category of sheaves
            on a
            %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/locale"} locale
            with respect to the
            %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/coverage"} topology
            of jointly epimorphic families (accordingly, every localic topos is a
            = succeed ")." do
              %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/Grothendieck+topos"} Grothendieck topos
          %p
            The
            %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/frame"} frame
            of
            %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/open+set"} opens
            specifying the locale may indeed be taken as the
            %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/poset"} poset
            of
            = succeed "s" do
              %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/subobject"} subobject
            %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "inline", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
              %semantics
                %mrow
                  %mn 1
                %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"} 1
            (i.e., internal
            = succeed ")." do
              %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/truth+value"} truth values
            = succeed "s" do
              %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/partial+equivalence+relation"} partial equivalence relation
            %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/tripos"} tripos
            given by a
            %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/complete+Heyting+algebra"} complete Heyting algebra
            (as before, the complete Heyting algebra may be taken as the poset of internal truth values).
          %h2#properties Properties
          %ul
            %li
              %p
                A Grothendieck topos
                %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "inline", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
                  %semantics
                    %mrow
                      %mi E
                    %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"} E
                is a localic topos if and only if its unique
                %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/global+section"} global section
                %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/geometric+morphism"} geometric morphism
                to
                %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/Set"} Set
                is a
                = succeed "." do
                  %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/localic+geometric+morphism"} localic geometric morphism
              %p
                Thus, in general we regard a localic geometric morphism
                %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "inline", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
                  %semantics
                    %mrow
                      %mi E
                      %mo →
                      %mi S
                    %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"} E \to S
                as exhibiting E as a “localic S-topos”.
            %li
              %p
                Moreover, just as localic topoi can be identified with locales, for any base topos
                %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "inline", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
                  %semantics
                    %mrow
                      %mi S
                    %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"} S
                the
                %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/2-category"} 2-category
                of localic
                = succeed "-topoi" do
                  %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "inline", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
                    %semantics
                      %mrow
                        %mi S
                      %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"} S
                %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/Loc"} Loc
                %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "inline", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
                  %semantics
                    %mrow
                      %mo{:stretchy => "false"} (
                      %mi S
                      %mo{:stretchy => "false"} )
                    %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"} (S)
                of
                %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/internalization"} internal
                = succeed "s" do
                  %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/locale"} locale
                = succeed "." do
                  %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "inline", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
                    %semantics
                      %mrow
                        %mi S
                      %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"} S
              .maruku-equation
                %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "block", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
                  %semantics
                    %mrow
                      %mi LocTopos
                      %mo{:stretchy => "false"} (
                      %mi S
                      %mo{:stretchy => "false"} )
                      %mo ≃
                      %mo{:stretchy => "false"} (
                      %mi Topos
                      %mo{:stretchy => "false"} /
                      %mi S
                      %msub
                        %mo{:stretchy => "false"} )
                        %mi loc
                      %mo ≃
                      %mi Loc
                      %mo{:stretchy => "false"} (
                      %mi S
                      %mo{:stretchy => "false"} )
                      %mspace{:width => "thinmathspace"}
                      %mo .
                    %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"}
                      LocTopos(S) \simeq (Topos/S)_{loc} \simeq Loc(S)
                      \\,.
              %p
                Here
                %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "inline", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
                  %semantics
                    %mrow
                      %mi LocTopos
                      %mo{:stretchy => "false"} (
                      %mi S
                      %mo{:stretchy => "false"} )
                    %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"} LocTopos(S)
                is the
                %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/2-category"} 2-category
                whose
              %ul
                %li
                  %p
                    objects are localic toposes over
                    = succeed ";" do
                      %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "inline", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
                        %semantics
                          %mrow
                            %mi S
                          %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"} S
                %li
                  %p
                    morphisms are
                    = succeed "s," do
                      %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/geometric+morphism"} geometric morphism
                    %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/adjunctions"} adjunctions
                    in which the left adjoint preserves
                    = succeed "," do
                      %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/finite+limits"} finite limits
                %li
                  %p
                    2-morphisms are
                    = succeed "-pairs" do
                      %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/mate"} mate
                    = succeed "s." do
                      %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/natural+transformation"} natural transformation
              %p
                Then the 2-category
                %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "inline", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
                  %semantics
                    %mrow
                      %mi LocTopos
                    %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"} LocTopos
                is
                %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/equivalence+of+categories"} equivalent
                to the 2-category
                %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "inline", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
                  %semantics
                    %mrow
                      %mi Loc
                    %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"} Loc
                of
                %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/locales"} locales
                (see C1.4.5 in the
                = succeed ")." do
                  %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/Elephant"} Elephant
              %p
                The 2-category
                %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "inline", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
                  %semantics
                    %mrow
                      %mi Loc
                    %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"} Loc
                is actually a
                = succeed ";" do
                  %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/%281%2C2%29-category"} (1,2)-category
                %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/2-morphism"} 2-morphism
                are the pointwise ordering of
                %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/frame"} frame
                homomorphisms. Thus this equivalence implies that
                %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "inline", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
                  %semantics
                    %mrow
                      %mi LocTopos
                    %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"} LocTopos
                is also a (1,2)-category, and moreover that it is
                = succeed "," do
                  %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/locally+small+category"} locally essentially small
                %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "inline", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
                  %semantics
                    %mrow
                      %mi Topos
                    %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"} Topos
                of all toposes is not locally essentially small.) Assuming sufficient separation axioms, the hom-posets of
                = succeed "," do
                  %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "inline", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
                    %semantics
                      %mrow
                        %mi Loc
                      %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"} Loc
                = succeed "," do
                  %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "inline", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
                    %semantics
                      %mrow
                        %mi LocTopos
                      %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"} LocTopos
          %h2#examples Examples
          %ul
            %li
              %p
                Obviously, every
                %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/Grothendieck+topos"} Grothendieck topos
                that is a
                %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/category+of+sheaves"} category of sheaves
                on (the
                %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/category+of+open+subsets"} category of open subsets
                of) a
                %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/topological+space"} topological space
                is localic.
            %li
              %p
                Every
                %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/sheaf+topos"} sheaf topos
                over a
                %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/posite"} posite
                is localic. (See there for details.)
          %p
            Many familiar toposes
            = succeed "," do
              %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "inline", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
                %semantics
                  %mrow
                    %mi E
                  %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"} E
            %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/over+topos"} slice
            %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "inline", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
              %semantics
                %mrow
                  %mi E
                  %mo{:stretchy => "false"} /
                  %mi X
                %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"} E/X
            (“covered” means the unique map
            %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "inline", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
              %semantics
                %mrow
                  %mi X
                  %mo →
                  %mn 1
                %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"} X \to 1
            is an epi). For example, if
            %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "inline", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
              %semantics
                %mrow
                  %mi G
                %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"} G
            is a group, then
            %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "inline", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
              %semantics
                %mrow
                  %mi E
                  %mo =
                  %msup
                    %mi Set
                    %mi G
                %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"} E = Set^G
            is not itself localic, but it has a localic slice
            %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "inline", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
              %semantics
                %mrow
                  %msup
                    %mi Set
                    %mi G
                  %mo{:stretchy => "false"} /
                  %mi G
                  %mo ≃
                  %mi Set
                %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"} Set^G/G \simeq Set
            that covers it. Such a topos is called an
            %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/%C3%A9tendue"} étendue
            (cf. Lawvere’s 1975 monograph
            = succeed ")." do
              %em Variable Sets Etendu and Variable Structure in Topoi
            %sup#fnref:1
              %a{:href => "#fn:1", :rel => "footnote"} 1
          %p
            A significant result due to Joyal and Tierney is that for any Grothendieck topos
            = succeed "," do
              %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "inline", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
                %semantics
                  %mrow
                    %mi E
                  %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"} E
            %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "inline", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
              %semantics
                %mrow
                  %mi F
                  %mo →
                  %mi E
                %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"} F \to E
            where
            %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "inline", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
              %semantics
                %mrow
                  %mi F
                %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"} F
            is localic. This fact is reproduced in Mac Lane and Moerdijk’s text
            %em Sheaves in Geometry and Logic
            (section IX.9), where the localic cover taken is called the
            %em Diaconescu cover
            of
            = succeed "." do
              %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "inline", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
                %semantics
                  %mrow
                    %mi E
                  %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"} E
          %p
            Then, using methods of descent theory, Joyal and Tierney deduce that every Grothendieck topos is equivalent to the category
            %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "inline", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
              %semantics
                %mrow
                  %mi B
                  %mi G
                %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"} B G
            of continuous discrete representations of a
            %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/localic+groupoid"} localic groupoid
            = succeed "." do
              %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "inline", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
                %semantics
                  %mrow
                    %mi G
                  %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"} G
            %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "inline", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
              %semantics
                %mrow
                  %mi S
                %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"} S
            as base.) This should be regarded as a major extrapolation of Grothendieck’s
            %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/Galois+theory"} Galois theory
            (as in
            %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/SGA"} SGA
            1), where it is shown that the
            %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/etale+topos"} etale topos
            of a field
            %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "inline", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
              %semantics
                %mrow
                  %mi k
                %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"} k
            is equivalent to the category of continuous discrete representations of the fundamental
            %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/pro-group"} pro-group
            = succeed "," do
              %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "inline", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
                %semantics
                  %mrow
                    %mi Gal
                    %mo{:stretchy => "false"} (
                    %mover
                      %mi k
                      %mo{:stretchy => "false"} ¯
                    %mo{:stretchy => "false"} /
                    %mi k
                    %mo{:stretchy => "false"} )
                  %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"} Gal(\bar{k}/k)
            %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "inline", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
              %semantics
                %mrow
                  %mover
                    %mi k
                    %mo{:stretchy => "false"} ¯
                %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"} \bar{k}
            denotes the separable closure of
            = succeed "." do
              %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "inline", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
                %semantics
                  %mrow
                    %mi k
                  %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"} k
          %h2#the_connection_with_propositional_logic The connection with propositional logic
          %p
            Recall that a
            %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/geometric+theory"} geometric theory
            %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "inline", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
              %semantics
                %mrow
                  %mi ℙ
                %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"} \mathbb{P}
            over a signature with no sort symbols is called
            = succeed "." do
              %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/propositional+theory"} propositional
            %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "inline", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
              %semantics
                %mrow
                  %mi ℙ
                %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"} \mathbb{P}
            admits only sequents over the empty context consisting of nested conjunctions or (infinitary) disjunctions of such relation symbols - in other words, logic boils down to
            = succeed "." do
              %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/propositional+logic"} propositional logic
          %p
            Propositional theories have the peculiarity that their classifying toposes always exist regardless of the availability of a
            %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/natural+numbers+object"} natural numbers object
            in the base topos (cf. for details&amp;references see at
            = succeed ")." do
              %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/classifying+topos"} classifying topos
          #localicclasifiespropositional.num_prop
            %h6#proposition Proposition
            %p
              Localic toposes correspond exactly to
              %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/classifying+topos"} classifying toposes
              of propositional theories.
          %p This appears in Johnstone (2002, D3.1.14, p.897f.).
          %p
            Given a locale
            = succeed "," do
              %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "inline", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
                %semantics
                  %mrow
                    %mi L
                  %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"} L
            %strong theory of completely prime filters
            %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "inline", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
              %semantics
                %mrow
                  %msub
                    %mi ℙ
                    %mi L
                %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"} \mathbb{P}_L
            has a 0-ary relation symbol
            %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "inline", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
              %semantics
                %mrow
                  %msub
                    %mi F
                    %mi x
                %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"} F_x
            for each
            = succeed "," do
              %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "inline", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
                %semantics
                  %mrow
                    %mi x
                    %mo ∈
                    %mi L
                  %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"} x\in L
            %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "inline", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
              %semantics
                %mrow
                  %mi x
                %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"} x
            is contained in the
            %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/filter"} filter
            = succeed "," do
              %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "inline", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
                %semantics
                  %mrow
                    %mi F
                  %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"} F
          %ul
            %li
              %p
                %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "inline", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
                  %semantics
                    %mrow
                      %mo ⊤
                      %mo ⊢
                      %msub
                        %mi F
                        %mn 1
                    %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"} \top\vdash F_1
                ,
            %li
              %p
                %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "inline", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
                  %semantics
                    %mrow
                      %msub
                        %mi F
                        %mi x
                      %mo ∧
                      %msub
                        %mi F
                        %mi y
                      %mo ⊢
                      %msub
                        %mi F
                        %mrow
                          %mi x
                          %mo ∧
                          %mi y
                    %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"} F_x\wedge F_y\vdash F_{x\wedge y}
                for all pairs
                = succeed "," do
                  %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "inline", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
                    %semantics
                      %mrow
                        %mi x
                        %mo ,
                        %mi y
                        %mo ∈
                        %mi L
                      %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"} x,y\in L
            %li
              %p
                %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "inline", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
                  %semantics
                    %mrow
                      %msub
                        %mi F
                        %mrow
                          %mo{:maxsize => "1.2em", :minsize => "1.2em"} (
                          %msub
                            %mo{:lspace => "thinmathspace", :rspace => "thinmathspace"} ⋁
                            %mrow
                              %mi i
                              %mo ∈
                              %mi I
                          %msub
                            %mi y
                            %mi i
                          %mo{:maxsize => "1.2em", :minsize => "1.2em"} )
                      %mo ⊢
                      %msub
                        %mo{:lspace => "thinmathspace", :rspace => "thinmathspace"} ⋁
                        %mrow
                          %mi i
                          %mo ∈
                          %mi I
                      %msub
                        %mi F
                        %mrow
                          %msub
                            %mi y
                            %mi i
                    %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"} F_{\big (\bigvee_{i\in I} y_i\big )}\vdash \bigvee_{i\in I} F_{y_i}
                \.
          %p
            In
            %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "inline", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
              %semantics
                %mrow
                  %mi Set
                %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"} Set
            models of
            %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "inline", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
              %semantics
                %mrow
                  %msub
                    %mi ℙ
                    %mi L
                %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"} \mathbb{P}_L
            correspond precisely to completely prime filters i.e. the multiplicatively closed subsets of
            %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "inline", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
              %semantics
                %mrow
                  %mi L
                %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"} L
            containing
            %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "inline", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
              %semantics
                %mrow
                  %mn 1
                %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"} 1
            that are inaccessible by infinite joins (
            %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "inline", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
              %semantics
                %mrow
                  %mo{:stretchy => "false"} (
                  %mo{:lspace => "thinmathspace", :rspace => "thinmathspace"} ⋁
                  %msub
                    %mi y
                    %mi i
                  %mo{:stretchy => "false"} )
                  %mo ∈
                  %mi F
                %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"} (\bigvee y_i)\in F
            implies
            %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "inline", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
              %semantics
                %mrow
                  %msub
                    %mi y
                    %mi i
                  %mo ∈
                  %mi F
                %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"} y_i\in F
            for some
            = succeed ")." do
              %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "inline", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
                %semantics
                  %mrow
                    %mi i
                  %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"} i
            %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "inline", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
              %semantics
                %mrow
                  %mn 0
                  %mo ∉
                  %mi F
                %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"} 0\notin F
            of a completely prime filter
            %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "inline", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
              %semantics
                %mrow
                  %mi F
                %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"} F
            is implicit in the third axiom schema with
            = succeed "." do
              %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "inline", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
                %semantics
                  %mrow
                    %mi I
                    %mo =
                    %mi ∅
                  %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"} I=\emptyset
          %p
            The relation between
            %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "inline", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
              %semantics
                %mrow
                  %mi L
                %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"} L
            and
            %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "inline", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
              %semantics
                %mrow
                  %msub
                    %mi ℙ
                    %mi L
                %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"} \mathbb{P}_L
            is that
            = succeed "." do
              %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "inline", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
                %semantics
                  %mrow
                    %mi Sh
                    %mo{:stretchy => "false"} (
                    %mi L
                    %mo{:stretchy => "false"} )
                    %mo ≃
                    %mi Set
                    %mo{:stretchy => "false"} [
                    %msub
                      %mi ℙ
                      %mi L
                    %mo{:stretchy => "false"} ]
                  %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"} Sh(L)\simeq Set[\mathbb{P}_L]
          %p
            Conversely, given a propositional theory
            = succeed "," do
              %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "inline", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
                %semantics
                  %mrow
                    %mi ℙ
                  %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"} \mathbb{P}
            %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/Lindenbaum-Tarski+algebra"} Lindenbaum-Tarski algebra
            of classes of provably equivalent formulas over
            %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "inline", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
              %semantics
                %mrow
                  %mi ℙ
                %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"} \mathbb{P}
            together with the entailment order yields a locale
            %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "inline", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
              %semantics
                %mrow
                  %msub
                    %mi L
                    %mi ℙ
                %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"} L_{\mathbb{P}}
            such that
            %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "inline", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
              %semantics
                %mrow
                  %mi Sh
                  %mo{:stretchy => "false"} (
                  %msub
                    %mi L
                    %mi ℙ
                  %mo{:stretchy => "false"} )
                %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"} Sh(L_\mathbb{P})
            classifies
            = succeed "." do
              %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "inline", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
                %semantics
                  %mrow
                    %mi ℙ
                  %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"} \mathbb{P}
          %h3#the_initial_and_terminal_toposes_as_classifying_toposes The initial and terminal toposes as classifying toposes
          %p
            For illustration let us consider the
            %em empty theory
            %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "inline", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
              %semantics
                %mrow
                  %msub
                    %mi 𝕋
                    %mn 1
                %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"} \mathbb{T}_1
            over the empty signature i.e
            %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "inline", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
              %semantics
                %mrow
                  %msub
                    %mi 𝕋
                    %mn 1
                %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"} \mathbb{T}_1
            has no axioms. This is certainly propositional, its deductive closure consists of all tautologies using
            = succeed "." do
              %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "inline", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
                %semantics
                  %mrow
                    %mo ⊥
                    %mo ,
                    %mo ⊤
                    %mo ,
                    %mo ∧
                    %mo ,
                    %mo{:lspace => "thinmathspace", :rspace => "thinmathspace"} ⋁
                  %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"} \bot,\top,\wedge,\bigvee
            %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "inline", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
              %semantics
                %mrow
                  %mstyle{:mathvariant => "bold"}
                    %mn 2
                  %mo ≃
                  %mo{:stretchy => "false"} {
                  %mo{:stretchy => "false"} [
                  %mo ⊥
                  %mo{:stretchy => "false"} ]
                  %mo ≤
                  %mo{:stretchy => "false"} [
                  %mo ⊤
                  %mo{:stretchy => "false"} ]
                  %mo{:stretchy => "false"} }
                %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"} \mathbf{2}\simeq\{[\bot]\leq[\top]\}
            which corresponds to the frame of open sets of the one-point space whence
            = succeed "." do
              %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "inline", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
                %semantics
                  %mrow
                    %mi Set
                    %mo{:stretchy => "false"} [
                    %msub
                      %mi 𝕋
                      %mn 1
                    %mo{:stretchy => "false"} ]
                    %mo =
                    %mi Sh
                    %mo{:stretchy => "false"} (
                    %mstyle{:mathvariant => "bold"}
                      %mn 2
                    %mo{:stretchy => "false"} )
                    %mo =
                    %mi Set
                  %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"} Set[\mathbb{T}_1]=Sh(\mathbf{2})=Set
            = succeed "," do
              %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "inline", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
                %semantics
                  %mrow
                    %msub
                      %mi ℙ
                      %mstyle{:mathvariant => "bold"}
                        %mn 1
                  %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"} \mathbb{P}_{\mathbf{1}}
            = succeed "," do
              %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "inline", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
                %semantics
                  %mrow
                    %mstyle{:mathvariant => "bold"}
                      %mn 1
                  %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"} \mathbf{1}
            %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "inline", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
              %semantics
                %mrow
                  %mi Sh
                  %mo{:stretchy => "false"} (
                  %mi ∅
                  %mo{:stretchy => "false"} )
                %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"} Sh(\emptyset)
            i.e. sheaves on the empty space.
          %p
            Whereas
            %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "inline", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
              %semantics
                %mrow
                  %msub
                    %mi ℙ
                    %mstyle{:mathvariant => "bold"}
                      %mn 2
                %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"} \mathbb{P}_{\mathbf{2}}
            has up to isomorphism exactly one model in every topos
            %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "inline", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
              %semantics
                %mrow
                  %mi ℰ
                %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"} \mathcal{E}
            namely the one interpreting
            %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "inline", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
              %semantics
                %mrow
                  %msub
                    %mi F
                    %mn 1
                %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"} F_1
            as
            %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "inline", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
              %semantics
                %mrow
                  %msub
                    %mi id
                    %mrow
                      %msub
                        %mn 1
                        %mi ℰ
                %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"} id_{1_\mathcal{E}}
            and
            %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "inline", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
              %semantics
                %mrow
                  %msub
                    %mi F
                    %mn 0
                %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"} F_0
            as
            = succeed "," do
              %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "inline", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
                %semantics
                  %mrow
                    %msub
                      %mn 0
                      %mi ℰ
                    %mo ↪
                    %msub
                      %mn 1
                      %mi ℰ
                  %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"} 0_{\mathcal{E}}\hookrightarrow 1_{\mathcal{E}}
            %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "inline", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
              %semantics
                %mrow
                  %msub
                    %mi ℙ
                    %mstyle{:mathvariant => "bold"}
                      %mn 1
                %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"} \mathbb{P}_{\mathbf{1}}
            has up to isomorphism exactly one model in up to isomorphism exactly one topos i.e. its model is the
            %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/zero+object"} zero object
            of the (necessarily) degenerate topos.
          %p
            Note that since
            %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "inline", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
              %semantics
                %mrow
                  %mi Set
                %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"} Set
            is the terminal Grothendieck topos, the empty theory
            %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "inline", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
              %semantics
                %mrow
                  %msub
                    %mi 𝕋
                    %mn 1
                %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"} \mathbb{T}_1
            over the empty signature is
            %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/Morita+equivalence"} Morita equivalent
            to any other geometric theory
            %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "inline", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
              %semantics
                %mrow
                  %mi 𝕋
                %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"} \mathbb{T}
            over any signature whatsoever provided
            %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "inline", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
              %semantics
                %mrow
                  %mi 𝕋
                %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"} \mathbb{T}
            has up to isomorphism exactly one model in every Grothendieck topos
            = succeed "." do
              %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "inline", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
                %semantics
                  %mrow
                    %mi ℰ
                  %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"} \mathcal{E}
            %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "inline", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
              %semantics
                %mrow
                  %mo{:stretchy => "false"} {
                  %mo ⊤
                  %mo ⊢
                  %mo ⊥
                  %mo{:stretchy => "false"} }
                %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"} \{\top\vdash\bot\}
            over the empty signature, namely adding a sort symbol
            %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "inline", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
              %semantics
                %mrow
                  %mi O
                %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"} O
            and “contextualising”
            %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "inline", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
              %semantics
                %mrow
                  %msub
                    %mi 𝕋
                    %mn 1
                  %mo ′
                  %mo =
                  %mo{:stretchy => "false"} {
                  %mo ⊤
                  %msub
                    %mo ⊢
                    %mrow
                      %mi x
                      %mo :
                      %mi O
                  %mo ⊥
                  %mo{:stretchy => "false"} }
                %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"} \mathbb{T}_1'=\{\top\vdash_{x:O}\bot\}
            has models exactly the initial objects but these are unique (and every topos has one) whence
            = succeed "." do
              %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "inline", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
                %semantics
                  %mrow
                    %mi Set
                    %mo{:stretchy => "false"} [
                    %msub
                      %mi 𝕋
                      %mn 1
                    %mo ′
                    %mo{:stretchy => "false"} ]
                    %mo ≃
                    %mi Set
                  %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"} Set[\mathbb{T}_1']\simeq Set
          %p
            Note also the difference in behavior between the inconsistent and the empty theory with respect to enlargening the signature: adding a sort symbol does not change the categories of models of the inconsistent theory up to isomorphism whereas
            %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "inline", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
              %semantics
                %mrow
                  %msub
                    %mi 𝕋
                    %mn 1
                  %mo ′
                %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"} \mathbb{T}_1'
            has entirely different categories of models from the
            %em empty theory
            over the signature containing a sort symbol
            %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "inline", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
              %semantics
                %mrow
                  %mi O
                %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"} O
            since the latter is the
            %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/theory+of+objects"} theory of objects
            = succeed ";" do
              %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "inline", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
                %semantics
                  %mrow
                    %mi 𝕆
                  %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"} \mathbb{O}
            %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "inline", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
              %semantics
                %mrow
                  %msub
                    %mi 𝕋
                    %mn 1
                  %mo ′
                %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"} \mathbb{T}_1'
            is a quotient of
            %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "inline", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
              %semantics
                %mrow
                  %mi 𝕆
                %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"} \mathbb{O}
            whence we can think of
            %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "inline", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
              %semantics
                %mrow
                  %msub
                    %mi 𝕋
                    %mn 1
                  %mo ′
                %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"} \mathbb{T}_1'
            as an axiomatisation of
            %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "inline", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
              %semantics
                %mrow
                  %mi Set
                %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"} Set
            as subtopos of
            %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "inline", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
              %semantics
                %mrow
                  %mi Set
                  %mo{:stretchy => "false"} [
                  %mi 𝕆
                  %mo{:stretchy => "false"} ]
                %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"} Set[\mathbb{O}]
            (see at
            %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/level"} level
            for further information on the duality between quotient theories and
            %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/subtopos"} subtoposes
            of the classifying topos). To sum up “paraphrasing” Tolstoy: there is only one way to be inconsistent but an infinite number of ways of being empty!
          %h2#generalizations Generalizations
          %p
            In the context of
            %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/%28%E2%88%9E%2C1%29-topos"} (∞,1)-topos
            %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/Higher+Topos+Theory"} theory
            there is a notion of
            = succeed "." do
              %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/n-localic+%28%E2%88%9E%2C1%29-topos"} n-localic (∞,1)-topos
          %p
            Notice that a
            %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/locale"} locale
            is itself a (Grothendieck)
            = succeed "." do
              %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/%280%2C1%29-topos"} (0,1)-topos
            %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/topos"} topos
            that behaves essentially like a
            = succeed "." do
              %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/%280%2C1%29-topos"} (0,1)-topos
            = succeed "." do
              %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/n-localic+%28infinity%2C1%29-topos"} 1-localic (1,1)-topos
          %h2#related_concepts Related concepts
          %ul
            %li
              %p
                %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/locale"} locale
            %li
              %p
                %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/localic+geometric+morphism"} localic geometric morphism
            %li
              %p
                %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/spatial+topos"}> spatial topos
                ,
                %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/category+of+sheaves+on+a+topological+space"} category of sheaves on a topological space
            %li
              %p
                %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/%C3%A9tendue"} étendue
            %li
              %p
                %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/n-localic+%28%E2%88%9E%2C1%29-topos"} n-localic (∞,1)-topos
            %li
              %p
                %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/n-localic+2-topos"} n-localic 2-topos
          %h2#references References
          %ul
            %li#MacLaneMoerdijk
              %p
                %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/Saunders+Mac+Lane"}> Saunders Mac Lane
                ,
                = succeed "," do
                  %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/Ieke+Moerdijk"} Ieke Moerdijk
                %em
                  %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/Sheaves+in+Geometry+and+Logic"} Sheaves in Geometry and Logic
                , Springer Heidelberg 1994. (chapter IX, p.472ff)
            %li
              %p
                %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/Peter+Johnstone"}> Peter Johnstone
                ,
                %em
                  %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/Sketches+of+an+Elephant"} Sketches of an Elephant
                  2 vols.
                , Oxford UP 2002. (In general around C.1.4.5 p.515f, cp filters at D1.1.7 p.816f, as classifying topos B4.2.12. p.431f, D1.1.14 p.897f.)
          .footnotes
            %hr/
            %ol
              %li#fn:1
                %p
                  The ‘etendu’ in the title of Lawvere’s monograph might not be a misspelled noun, but an adjective as part of a back translation of a (hypothetical) French expression ‘ensembles étendus’. See this
                  %a{:href => "http://nforum.mathforge.org/discussion/5805/etendue/"} nForum thread
                  for some discussion and speculation on this point.
                  %a{:href => "#fnref:1", :rev => "footnote"} ↩
